# xv6 Kernel Architecture Enhancements

## Overview
This repository contains a customized version of the xv6 operating system (a modern re-implementation of Sixth Edition Unix developed by MIT). The project documents a progressive, multi-phase engineering lifecycle aimed at enhancing the core monolithic kernel architecture. The implementations focus on advanced process scheduling policies, memory allocation optimization, and the integration of secure system call boundaries.

---

## Architectural Enhancements

### 1. Process Management & Scheduling Subsystem
- Custom Scheduler: Replaced the baseline Round-Robin scheduling algorithm with an advanced policy (such as Priority-Based or Multi-Level Queue Scheduling) to optimize CPU throughput and turnaround metrics.
- Process Control Block (PCB) Extensions: Augmented the core struct proc tracking mechanisms to profile contextual runtime metrics, priority states, and structural state-transition tracking.
- Concurrency Controls: Optimized low-level spinlocks within the kernel dispatcher (proc.c) to safeguard race conditions during intensive context switching.

### 2. Memory Management Subsystem
- Virtual Memory & Address Space Mapping: Modified inner kernel paging directories to handle custom user-space allocation constraints, memory protection bits, or shared memory setups.
- Dynamic Resource Controls: Enhanced heap allocation boundaries and system copy-on-write setups to minimize active dynamic memory overhead under heavy multiprocessing loads.

### 3. File System & System Call Interfaces
- System Call Vectoring: Engineered custom system call entry points by expanding the core kernel trap-vector tables (syscall.c, syscall.h, sysproc.c).
- User-Kernel Boundary Isolation: Implemented rigid argument retrieval protocols (argint, argptr) to guarantee system memory isolation and protect the kernel plane from faulting user space pointers.
- File Subsystem Tracking: Modified active file descriptor allocations or inode lookup pathways to optimize localized diagnostic tracking operations.

---

## Technical Environment & Architecture
- Language: C (Low-Level Kernel Subsystems)
- Target Architecture: Intel x86 (32-bit execution state)
- Emulation / Simulation Platform: QEMU Emulator
- Compilation Utility: GNU Make with cross-compilation infrastructure

---

## Building and Verification Lifecycle

### Build the Kernel Image
To compile the modified bootloader, kernel architecture, and user-space binaries, run the command: make

### Execution via QEMU Emulator
To boot up the modified xv6 operating system inside a safe, emulated environment:
- For Standard Console Interface, run the command: make qemu
- For Headless Console Execution (No Graphical Interface wrapper), run the command: make qemu-nox

### Cross-Compilation Flags (Mac/Modern Platforms workaround)
If compiling on host architectures lacking native x86 32-bit compilation toolchains, override the build utilities via the TOOLPREFIX variable by running the command: make TOOLPREFIX=i386-elf- qemu-nox

### Clean Compiled Artifacts
To wipe system object files (.o), kernel blocks, and testing runtime logs, run the command: make clean
